# The three-valued X = Y of balls with 2-limb centers (milestone2.md, "One enclosure interface",
# "Comparisons": certain): yes when both balls are the same point, r_x = r_y = 0 and c_x = c_y by
# eq_2_2, which is exact; no when the balls are disjoint, s < al, al = RD(to_down_2(abs_2(z)) - e),
# written as minus the rounding up of the opposite, a finite lower bound of |c_y - c_x|, and s =
# radd(r_x, r_y) an upper bound of r_x + r_y. Never both. Both false for an empty operand and for
# the whole line. The yes is exact; the no is certain, not exact.
kernel:        ball_equal_2
input:         cx = cx0 cx1
input:         stored rx
input:         cy = cy0 cy1
input:         stored ry
output:        mask yes
output:        mask no
requires:      ball cx rx
requires:      ball cy ry
ensures:       trit yes no eq x y x=cx,rx y=cy,ry
min_precision: 3
source:        the radii and the centers compared, and a bound of the sum of the radii with a bound of the distance of the centers (Milestone 2 listing)
license:       algorithm and proof: MIT
status:        unverified
domain:        extended
ieee_status:   unverified
entry:         yes
cone_exempt:   the results are masks, which have no non-finite value, and a comparison with NaN is false; trit states what comes out for every canonical ball

ea          = eq(rx, 0)
eb          = eq(ry, 0)
ec          = eq_2_2(cx0, cx1, cy0, cy1)
eab         = and(ea, eb)
yes         = and(eab, ec)
ncx0        = neg(cx0)
ncx1        = neg(cx1)
(z0, z1, e) = add_ball_2_2(cy0, cy1, ncx0, ncx1)
s           = radd(rx, ry)
(w0, w1)    = abs_2(z0, z1)
wd          = to_down_2(w0, w1)
nw          = neg(wd)
tl          = add_up_1(e, nw)
al          = neg(tl)
aa          = abs(al)
fa          = lt(aa, const inf)
b           = lt(s, al)
no          = and(b, fa)
